  <Target Name="CopyGameConfig" AfterTargets="Build">
    <!-- The deployment layout Balance::FindConfigFile looks for first: Config next to
         the executable. Without this the exe only finds a config by walking up into
         the repository, so a copy taken anywhere else silently runs on the built in
         defaults.

         This had Inputs/Outputs once and it was wrong, for the reason the bake target
         below spells out: timestamps only answer "is the source newer", and a source
         can go backwards. A git checkout or a restored backup left an older original
         beside a newer copy, the copy was kept, and the copy is the one the game
         reads. That is the whole bug this work is about, sitting in the build.

         SkipUnchangedFiles still makes the ordinary case free. It compares size and
         write time rather than asking which is newer, so it copies whenever the two
         differ in either direction. -->
    <Copy SourceFiles="$(SolutionDir)Config\GameBalance.ini" DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)Config" SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
  </Target>
  <Target Name="CopyGameAssets" AfterTargets="Build">
    <!-- Same deployment rule as the config, and the same trap with it: a stale copy
         beside the executable is found before a fresh one in the repository, so
         replacing a wav without building leaves the old sound playing. No
         Inputs/Outputs here because the set of files changes as assets are added,
         and SkipUnchangedFiles already makes the repeat case cheap. -->
    <ItemGroup>
      <GameSoundAssets Include="$(SolutionDir)Assets\Sound\*.wav" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <Copy SourceFiles="@(GameSoundAssets)" DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)Assets\Sound" SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
  </Target>
  <Target Name="BakeBalanceDefaults" BeforeTargets="ClCompile">
    <!-- The built-in defaults, generated from the shipped config instead of typed a
         second time in Balance.h.

         They used to be two hand-written copies of the same game, and the header
         comment said so and asked people to keep them in step. People did not, twice
         in one day: a tuned ini against stale defaults, and then a budget constant
         that made every seed unbuildable. The self test caught both, which is the
         point of it, but catching a mistake is worse than not being able to make it.

         Raw string literal, so quotes and backslashes in the ini need no escaping.
         The delimiter has to be one the file cannot contain, and ")BALANCEINI" is
         not a thing an ini says.

         No Inputs/Outputs on purpose. Timestamp comparison asks whether the input is
         newer than the output, and a file can go backwards in time: a git checkout, a
         revert, a restored backup. Any of those leaves the baked copy stale while
         MSBuild reports the target up to date, which is the same "the file you
         edited is not the file being read" this whole thing exists to end, one level
         further in. Reading a seven kilobyte ini every build costs nothing, and
         WriteOnlyWhenDifferent means nothing downstream rebuilds unless the contents
         actually moved. -->
    <PropertyGroup>
      <BalanceIniText>$([System.IO.File]::ReadAllText('$(SolutionDir)Config\GameBalance.ini'))</BalanceIniText>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <MakeDir Directories="$(IntDir)" />
    <WriteLinesToFile File="$(IntDir)GameBalanceDefaults.g.h" Overwrite="true" WriteOnlyWhenDifferent="true"
                      Lines="// Generated from Config/GameBalance.ini by the BakeBalanceDefaults target.%0D%0A// Do not edit: edit the ini.%0D%0A%23pragma once%0D%0A%0D%0Anamespace Zombie%0D%0A{%0D%0A    inline constexpr char DefaultBalanceIni[] = R&quot;BALANCEINI(%0D%0A$(BalanceIniText)%0D%0A)BALANCEINI&quot;%3B%0D%0A}%0D%0A" />
  </Target>
